---
import Alert from '@ds/components/feedback/Alert.astro';
import Table from '@ds/components/ui/Table.astro';
// The sample is a real configuration, checked with `terraform validate` against the provider by `typecheck`.
import mainTf from '../../../samples/terraform/main.tf?raw';
import CodeSample from '../../components/CodeSample.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const build = `pnpm nx run @ctff/terraform-provider:build
# → apps/terraform-provider-ctff/dist/terraform-provider-ctff`;

const devOverride = `provider_installation {
  dev_overrides {
    "kevinverreault/ctff" = "/path/to/capture-the-feature-flag/apps/terraform-provider-ctff/dist"
  }
  direct {}
}`;

const resources = [
  { resource: 'ctff_project', arguments: 'key, name', computed: 'id', import: '<project>' },
  { resource: 'ctff_environment', arguments: 'project_key, key, name', computed: 'id, sdk_key (sensitive)', import: '<project>/<env>' },
  { resource: 'ctff_flag', arguments: 'project_key, key, name, description, tags (set), archived', computed: 'id, version, managed_by', import: '<project>/<flag>' },
  {
    resource: 'ctff_flag_environment',
    arguments: 'project_key, flag_key, environment_key, on, off_variation, default_variation, individual_targets, rules',
    computed: 'id, version',
    import: '<project>/<flag>/<env>',
  },
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Terraform guide"
  description="Manage projects, environments, flags and targeting as code with the Capture The Feature Flag Terraform provider."
>
  <section class="section">
    <h1 class="ff-serif text-3xl">Terraform guide</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="text-lg">
        The <code>kevinverreault/ctff</code> provider manages projects, environments, flags and their targeting as
        code. Like every other client, it talks only to Core’s <code>/api/v1</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">Install the provider</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        V1 publishes nothing to a registry, so build the provider from the repository (it needs Go) and point
        Terraform at the build with a dev override in <code>~/.terraformrc</code>, or in the file named by
        <code>TF_CLI_CONFIG_FILE</code>. With a dev override, skip <code>terraform init</code> and run
        <code>terraform plan</code> and <code>apply</code> directly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeSample lang="bash" title="Build" code={build} />
    <CodeSample lang="hcl" title="~/.terraformrc" code={devOverride} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Or let compose do it: <code>docker compose --profile terraform up</code> builds the provider in an image
        and applies the example configuration of the Birdwatch demo to the stack’s Core.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">Declare a flag and its targeting</h2>
    <CodeSample lang="hcl" title="main.tf" code={mainTf} />
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Variations are indexes</strong> into the flag’s variations, <code>[true, false]</code> for every
          V1 flag: <code>0</code> serves <code>true</code> and <code>1</code> serves <code>false</code>.
          <code>off_variation</code> defaults to <code>1</code> and <code>default_variation</code> to
          <code>0</code>, as for a new flag.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>individual_targets</code></strong> is a set of <code>{'{ variation, user_ids }'}</code>, at
          most one per variation. <strong><code>rules</code></strong> is an ordered list of
          <code>{'{ attribute, operator, values, variation }'}</code>, where <code>attribute</code> is
          <code>user_id</code> or <code>country</code> and <code>operator</code> is <code>in</code> or
          <code>not_in</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Targeting always exists</strong>: Core gives every flag targeting in every environment, so a
          <code>ctff_flag_environment</code> takes it over. Destroying the resource puts back the off state of a new
          flag.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plan checks what Core would reject</strong>: keys (lowercase kebab-case), variation indexes,
          operators and attributes, country codes and empty user IDs.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <Table caption="Resources" columns={[
      { key: 'resource', label: 'Resource' },
      { key: 'arguments', label: 'Arguments' },
      { key: 'computed', label: 'Computed' },
      { key: 'import', label: 'Import ID' },
    ]} rows={resources} rowHeader="resource" />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">Working alongside the admin app</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Managed by Terraform.</strong> The provider marks its flags as managed by
          <code>terraform</code>, so the admin app shows a badge on them and warns before they are edited. Nothing is
          locked.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drift.</strong> Edits made in the admin app show up on the next plan, and applying puts the
          declared configuration back.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Versions.</strong> Flag and targeting changes send the version Terraform last read. If someone
          changed it between plan and apply, Core answers <code>409 Conflict</code> and the apply fails instead of
          overwriting their change.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audit entries.</strong> Every request carries <code>X-CTFF-Actor: terraform</code>, the actor Core
          records on the audit entry of each change.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Archiving.</strong> <code>archived = true</code> archives a flag: it is no longer served, so SDKs
          get their fallback value. Setting it back to <code>false</code> restores it. Destroying a
          <code>ctff_flag</code> archives it, then deletes it permanently.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <Alert variant="warning" icon="triangle-alert" title="Keys are immutable">
      Changing a <code>key</code> or <code>project_key</code> replaces the resource. Replacing a flag deletes its
      targeting in every environment, and replacing an environment gives it a new SDK key.
    </Alert>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
